```java
interface VendingState {
    VendingState selectProduct(Machine m, String slotCode);
    VendingState insertMoney(Machine m, int cents);
    VendingState dispense(Machine m);
    VendingState cancel(Machine m);
}

class IdleState implements VendingState {
    public VendingState selectProduct(Machine m, String slotCode) {
        m.setSelectedSlot(slotCode);
        return new SelectingState();
    }
    public VendingState insertMoney(Machine m, int cents) { return this; } // nothing selected yet
    public VendingState dispense(Machine m) { return this; }
    public VendingState cancel(Machine m) { return this; }
}
```

Each method returns the *next* state instead of mutating a shared enum — `Machine.selectProduct()`
just calls `state = state.selectProduct(this, code)`. Every state class only implements the
transitions actually legal from it; `IdleState.insertMoney` is a no-op by design, not a bug,
because there's nothing to pay for yet.

```java
class ChangeMaker {
    // DP coin change against real available inventory, not a fixed denomination list
    static int[] makeChange(int amountCents, Map<Integer, Integer> availableCoins) {
        int[] denoms = availableCoins.keySet().stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sorted().toArray();
        int[] minCoins = new int[amountCents + 1];
        Arrays.fill(minCoins, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        minCoins[0] = 0;
        for (int c = 1; c <= amountCents; c++) {
            for (int d : denoms) {
                if (d <= c && minCoins[c - d] != Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                    minCoins[c] = Math.min(minCoins[c], minCoins[c - d] + 1);
                }
            }
        }
        if (minCoins[amountCents] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) throw new ExactChangeUnavailableException();
        return minCoins;
    }
}
```

`ChangeMaker` is a standalone class the machine *calls*, not a method on `Machine` or any
`VendingState` — change-making is a pure algorithm problem, and testing it shouldn't require
standing up a state machine.
